WEBVTT

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:07.980
Well, we didn't have a regular practice today.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.700
Coach gave us a lecture on how to lose with a smile.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:14.280
How you doing, Mr. Willett?

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:18.200
Fine. Tell us how to spit on the ring today.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:43.160
beaver starring Barbara Billingsley Hugh Beaumont Tony Dow and Jerry Mathers as

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:59.980
the beaver I got home after you thought you'd gone to bed last

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.760
How'd you make out with the baseball tryouts?

00:01:02.170 --> 00:01:02.910
Oh, great, Dad.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:05.950
The coach says right now I'm practically first-string pitcher.

00:01:06.410 --> 00:01:08.090
Well, that's swell, Wally.

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:09.680
Hey, did you hear that, June?

00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:10.880
First-string pitcher.

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:12.460
How about you, Beaver?

00:01:12.970 --> 00:01:13.990
Did you go out for the C-team?

00:01:15.960 --> 00:01:16.760
Oh, sure, Dad.

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:19.260
Coach, do each guy two flies.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:21.410
I caught the first one.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:23.860
The second one hit me on the head.

00:01:24.370 --> 00:01:25.210
On the head?

00:01:25.490 --> 00:01:29.260
I knew something like this would happen.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:32.500
He hit me real hard, but I didn't cry, though.

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:33.890
What did the coach say?

00:01:35.320 --> 00:01:37.000
He said I wasn't good enough for the team.

00:01:37.930 --> 00:01:41.340
If I didn't know that, I wouldn't have bothered not to cry.

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:43.460
Well, beaver.

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:45.930
Hey, Dad, it's gonna be real neat.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:49.380
We get to wear uniforms and get to ride on buses to games and everything.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.040
Well, Wally, I'm really proud of you.

00:01:52.690 --> 00:01:54.990
Hey, I'll tell you what, after I get home from work tonight,

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:57.170
if you want to, we'll go over to the vacant lot and play some catch.

00:01:57.580 --> 00:01:58.820
Gee, that'd be great, Dad.

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:04.620
Well, we'll be using a hardball, Beaver.

00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:06.680
I expect you better just hit the homework.

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:13.760
Oh, Beaver, I wouldn't be upset about not making the team.

00:02:14.460 --> 00:02:16.360
There are lots of other things you can do at school.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.180
Sure, there are.

00:02:19.380 --> 00:02:21.760
But anyway, you're gonna be a ball player one of these days.

00:02:22.130 --> 00:02:22.790
That's right.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:26.720
And in the meantime, well, there must be something else you'd like to do.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:29.960
Something that... something that is...

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:39.080
appeals to you. Well, I like to wait in big puddles, but I don't think they have a

00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:57.480
team for that. Have a good day at the office? Miserable. That's nice. Oh dear, there's

00:02:57.480 --> 00:02:59.860
something over here from the boys' school on the desk.

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:04.500
If it's their report cards, I'd better wait till I'm in a better humor.

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.600
It's permission for the Beaver to take music lessons.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.360
Music? Uh-huh.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.420
She's never been interested in music before.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:16.600
Well, I guess he was so disappointed about not making the baseball team,

00:03:16.600 --> 00:03:17.740
he wanted to go out for something.

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:20.980
At least there's nothing in the band to hit him on the head.

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:23.140
I wouldn't be too sure of that.

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:26.340
You really going out for the band, Beaver?

00:03:27.400 --> 00:03:29.970
Yeah, I get to wear a uniform, and for $5...

00:03:32.140 --> 00:03:41.400
They got all kinds of instruments here.

00:03:42.140 --> 00:03:43.080
Which one are you going to play?

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:44.810
I don't know.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:48.270
Hard to tell by looking which one is the easiest.

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:56.040
That?

00:03:56.440 --> 00:03:57.340
Well, that's a tuba.

00:03:58.800 --> 00:03:59.160
Tuba.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.260
You're too small for that, Beaver.

00:04:01.890 --> 00:04:04.350
And anyway, Tui's been playing the tuba since the fifth grade.

00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:06.260
Doesn't he have to polish it all the time?

00:04:06.570 --> 00:04:09.050
Nah, he gets his mom to do it for him.

00:04:10.150 --> 00:04:15.350
That looks good.

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:16.130
What's that?

00:04:17.020 --> 00:04:17.840
That's a clarinet.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:21.690
I'll go out for that.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:24.280
It looks easy to carry.

00:04:26.910 --> 00:04:28.910
And it's made of wood, so I won't have to polish it.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:31.910
I sure hope it sounds good.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:37.520
Well, supper's ready, boys. You'd better get washed up.

00:04:38.100 --> 00:04:38.640
Okay, Dad.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:47.240
I understand you're going to take some music lessons at school, Beaver.

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:50.590
Yeah, I think I'm going to go out for the clarinet.

00:04:50.790 --> 00:04:53.950
I don't know how it sounds, but I like the way it looks.

00:04:54.430 --> 00:04:55.430
Well, that's fine.

00:04:56.360 --> 00:04:59.960
I do hope, though, that it's not going to turn out like the...

00:05:00.520 --> 00:05:04.410
you had last year. Remember, you quit after one week.

00:05:05.290 --> 00:05:08.320
Oh, no. I don't think I'll fall down playing the clarinet.

00:05:10.120 --> 00:05:11.380
I don't think so, either.

00:05:11.950 --> 00:05:14.460
I, uh, just hope you stick with it, that's all.

00:05:15.180 --> 00:05:15.940
Oh, sure, Dad.

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:25.620
Hey, B. How are you doing the clarinet?

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:29.120
Hey, Wally. They taught us how to take it apart today.

00:05:37.460 --> 00:05:38.120
Baseball, Wally.

00:05:38.700 --> 00:05:40.600
Well, we didn't have a regular practice today.

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:43.320
Coach gave us a lecture on how to lose with a smile.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:46.920
How you doing, Mr. Willett?

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:48.130
Fine.

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:50.830
That's how I spit on the ring today.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:55.550
Um, hi, Mom.

00:05:55.670 --> 00:05:57.110
I've been worried about you two.

00:05:58.170 --> 00:05:59.970
Oh, we ate dinner with the team on the way home from Bedford.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.660
Good. I, uh, take it you won?

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.410
You bet we did. The old pro here only allowed 12 runs.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:09.700
That good?

00:06:10.390 --> 00:06:12.150
Well, it is when your team's scoring 14.

00:06:13.250 --> 00:06:15.450
Hey, what's it, Beaver? I want to tell him all about the game.

00:06:15.590 --> 00:06:17.130
He's been waiting for you to come home.

00:06:17.440 --> 00:06:20.360
This is a big night for him. He's gonna play something for us.

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.430
Want the clarinet? Uh-huh. Oh, boy, I gotta hear this.

00:06:23.730 --> 00:06:26.590
You know, he looks like he's been doing this all his life.

00:06:27.090 --> 00:06:28.570
And after only five lessons.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:39.860
Come on, Peef, will you quit showing off and play something?

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:41.680
Now be quiet, Wally.

00:07:02.270 --> 00:07:07.620
Well, that was very nice, Beaver. Wasn't it, Ward?

00:07:08.920 --> 00:07:11.160
Oh, yes. Yes, it certainly was.

00:07:12.410 --> 00:07:15.390
As a matter of fact, it sounded vaguely familiar.

00:07:15.730 --> 00:07:16.710
Well, of course it did.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:18.200
It's America.

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:25.350
Well, sure, of course it was America.

00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:29.880
Hey, B, play it again. Sounds like there's something missing.

00:07:47.760 --> 00:07:53.890
It's a miracle, but Dave, tell me, why do you always stop there?

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:55.680
Because that's how far I'm memorized.

00:07:56.590 --> 00:07:59.890
Mr. Wilt says, as soon as I learn the last few notes, I can go on with the other guys.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:02.290
Oh, well, that's fine.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:06.150
I'm sure glad you decided to stick with it, Beeve.

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:08.400
You want to come upstairs and watch me take it apart?

00:08:09.390 --> 00:08:11.630
Well, why don't you fellas go on up now?

00:08:11.730 --> 00:08:12.530
I'll be up a little later.

00:08:13.560 --> 00:08:14.100
Okay, Dad.

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:22.620
Wally, you didn't say nothing.

00:08:22.990 --> 00:08:23.830
How'd you like it?

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:27.190
Well, for a guy that just went out to spit on the reed a couple days ago,

00:08:27.630 --> 00:08:28.530
I thought it was pretty good.

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:40.220
Oh, boy, isn't he cute with that clarinet?

00:08:40.640 --> 00:08:43.880
You gotta give him credit. He certainly thinks he can play it, doesn't he?

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:52.720
Four yesterday, and my mother says if I don't make it, she's gonna phone Mr. Willett.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.500
Mr. Willett told me I got a pretty good chance.

00:08:56.550 --> 00:08:59.830
Mr. Willett didn't tell me anything, but I think I got a pretty good chance, too.

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.840
You, Beaver, Mr. Willis has just given you lessons

00:09:02.840 --> 00:09:04.460
because he had an extra clarinet.

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:06.600
You're awful, isn't he, Whitey?

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:08.020
He's not really awful.

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.130
He just sounds that way when he plays.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:14.610
You just wait and see what happens this afternoon, Beaver.

00:09:14.790 --> 00:09:16.010
You just wait and see.

00:10:09.180 --> 00:10:12.120
Yes, I think with a little work, that'll be fine, Judy.

00:10:12.360 --> 00:10:13.540
Thank you, Mr. Willett.

00:10:24.470 --> 00:10:25.410
Bye, Mr. Willett.

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:26.040
Bye, Judy.

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:28.540
All right, now, Herbert?

00:10:40.330 --> 00:10:40.950
Excuse me.

00:10:41.210 --> 00:10:59.890
Thank you Tom.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:03.140
I don't believe we're quite ready for the orchestra just yet.

00:11:03.450 --> 00:11:05.010
Perhaps with a little more practice.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:05.270
Hmm?

00:11:06.330 --> 00:11:15.930
That's gonna be very...

00:11:28.890 --> 00:11:29.970
That's fine, why...

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.160
You're making real progress.

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:32.690
Thank you, Mr. Woollett.

00:11:33.580 --> 00:11:34.940
I practiced a lot at home,

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.410
except when my big brother hits me.

00:11:38.650 --> 00:11:39.870
Well, you keep it up, Whitey.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:42.430
I think you'll be a credit to our woodwinds.

00:11:44.100 --> 00:11:47.000
Bye, Mr. Woollett.

00:11:47.220 --> 00:11:47.700
All right.

00:11:48.070 --> 00:11:49.870
Now, Theodore, let's hear what you can do.

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:51.790
Right now?

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:52.980
Right now.

00:11:56.060 --> 00:11:58.860
We were working on America, weren't we?

00:11:59.430 --> 00:11:59.970
Yes, ma'am.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:18.260
Where can't you go any further?

00:12:19.520 --> 00:12:20.780
Could but it wouldn't be America

00:12:20.780 --> 00:12:25.450
We'll see

00:12:32.560 --> 00:12:34.570
Oh, Lord, Mr. Wood, really I will.

00:12:39.560 --> 00:12:40.760
Temphing the band, huh?

00:12:42.830 --> 00:12:51.520
Well, you see, Theodore, you and Whitey are the youngest that came out for the orchestra.

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:56.960
And Whitey's been taking lessons for six months, so naturally he's quite far ahead of you.

00:13:00.160 --> 00:13:00.960
Maybe next year.

00:13:02.270 --> 00:13:08.330
Uh, maybe I could just sit in the bed and pretend like I was playing?

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.240
Wouldn't be fair either to you or the other children, now would it?

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:15.710
I guess not.

00:13:18.470 --> 00:13:19.890
Do I have to get back the clarinet?

00:13:21.000 --> 00:13:24.160
Well, Theodore, I'll be working with the others every day after school.

00:13:25.500 --> 00:13:29.760
But I guess you can keep it for a while and you can practice at home.

00:13:30.320 --> 00:13:31.240
Thank you, Mr. Wood.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:37.720
How'd you do, Bee?

00:13:38.820 --> 00:13:39.460
Who, me?

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:41.310
Yeah, you had trials today.

00:13:42.280 --> 00:13:43.840
Oh, great, Wally, just great.

00:13:44.110 --> 00:13:44.610
Oh, good.

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:56.390
Hi, Beaver. Hi, Mom.

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:58.340
Wally still at school?

00:13:59.620 --> 00:13:59.900
Yeah.

00:14:00.960 --> 00:14:02.040
He had to practice baseball.

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:03.370
Oh, that's right.

00:14:03.470 --> 00:14:05.550
Your father's going to stop by and watch them play.

00:14:06.150 --> 00:14:07.170
Yeah, I see him there.

00:14:07.590 --> 00:14:07.790
Oh?

00:14:09.160 --> 00:14:11.440
How come you aren't at school practicing with a band?

00:14:13.320 --> 00:14:16.620
Uh, I was at school early today.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:17.320
Oh.

00:14:19.770 --> 00:14:20.830
Honey, don't spoil your dinner.

00:14:21.300 --> 00:14:21.880
Oh, no, Mom.

00:14:25.220 --> 00:14:29.970
Mom, my dad's pretty glad about why I make a...

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:31.080
on the baseball team, huh?

00:14:31.770 --> 00:14:32.790
He certainly is.

00:14:34.360 --> 00:14:37.610
And I guess he's kind of glad about me and the clarinet, too.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:40.710
Oh, he certainly is, Beaver.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:43.000
Well, you're only in the second grade,

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.160
and to be in the school band, that's quite an accomplishment.

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:54.210
Yeah, and I guess Dad would feel pretty bad

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:57.770
if Wally was no good and got thrown off the team.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:14:59.980
Yes, yes he is.

00:15:00.190 --> 00:15:02.640
He'd be quite upset.

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:04.800
I think so.

00:15:07.910 --> 00:15:11.320
Beaver, Wally hasn't gotten into any trouble

00:15:11.320 --> 00:15:12.700
on the baseball team, has he?

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:14.180
Oh, no, Mom.

00:15:15.020 --> 00:15:16.620
With Billy Ehart having the measles,

00:15:17.480 --> 00:15:19.380
Wally's just about the best player in the whole school.

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:22.230
Well, then why all this talk?

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:24.140
I don't know.

00:15:25.310 --> 00:15:27.190
I guess I'd just like to suppose things

00:15:27.190 --> 00:15:28.070
that aren't gonna happen.

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:39.580
You're gonna be late.

00:15:41.880 --> 00:15:43.640
Good long, Mom. Good lunch.

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:45.380
Good long, Dad. Good long, Wally.

00:15:45.780 --> 00:15:47.580
Uh, say, hey, uh, you gonna pitch again today?

00:15:47.890 --> 00:15:49.730
Yeah, Dad. Mom feels great.

00:15:49.930 --> 00:15:50.730
I saw it.

00:15:55.860 --> 00:15:56.440
Get your clarinet.

00:16:01.370 --> 00:16:02.540
I almost forgot it.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:05.100
Hey, Beaver, how you coming along with the band?

00:16:06.000 --> 00:16:06.660
Oh, fine.

00:16:07.360 --> 00:16:08.540
My fingers feel great.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:09.460
Smile.

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:11.500
Bye.

00:16:11.980 --> 00:16:12.220
Bye.

00:16:18.520 --> 00:16:22.980
Ward, I know you're happy about Wally and the baseball team,

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.870
but, well, I think you should make a little more fuss maybe

00:16:25.870 --> 00:16:27.330
over Beaver and his clarinet.

00:16:28.550 --> 00:16:29.970
Well, Joan, I'm proud of you.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.680
as can be about him being in the band.

00:16:32.380 --> 00:16:34.080
After all, he's only in the second grade.

00:16:35.010 --> 00:16:37.630
Look, I'll tell you what, tonight we'll ask him to play for us again.

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:39.710
Ought to show a lot of improvement after two weeks, huh?

00:16:53.060 --> 00:16:54.860
Now, Wally, I wouldn't laugh at your brother.

00:16:55.220 --> 00:16:57.880
I think he's shown remarkable progress.

00:16:58.180 --> 00:16:59.810
Don't you, June? Oh, yes.

00:17:00.670 --> 00:17:01.370
Yes, I do.

00:17:02.070 --> 00:17:04.340
Thank you. Could I go now?

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:05.040
Certainly.

00:17:05.650 --> 00:17:06.550
I'd better go, too.

00:17:09.860 --> 00:17:15.970
Hey, June, I didn't want to say anything to discourage him,

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:18.070
but isn't that the same tune he played two weeks ago?

00:17:18.640 --> 00:17:22.500
Yes, it is. Maybe that's Mr. Willett's system.

00:17:25.980 --> 00:17:29.220
Yeah, maybe when he gets the whole band together, it comes out all right.

00:17:31.980 --> 00:17:35.000
You think I ought to call him up and just see how the Beeb's doing?

00:17:35.920 --> 00:17:37.300
Oh, no, Ward, I wouldn't do that.

00:17:37.480 --> 00:17:39.740
I'd hate him to think we were like Judy's mother.

00:17:52.160 --> 00:17:54.520
Hey, Beeb, that junk you just played,

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:57.330
it was the same junk you played before, wasn't it?

00:17:58.740 --> 00:17:59.640
Maybe it was.

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.310
All right, Beeve. When did they kick you out of the band?

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:07.870
Two weeks ago.

00:18:08.500 --> 00:18:11.320
And you've been carrying that thing back and forth just to make it look good.

00:18:11.900 --> 00:18:15.380
What a goof. Can't get away with it, pretending you're in the band.

00:18:15.930 --> 00:18:17.770
They're gonna catch you and then you're gonna get in trouble.

00:18:18.520 --> 00:18:20.780
Well, if I do get in trouble, it's all your fault.

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:22.050
My fault?

00:18:22.730 --> 00:18:24.130
Yeah, if I had to have a brother,

00:18:24.630 --> 00:18:27.150
why did it have to be one that could play baseball so good?

00:18:32.200 --> 00:18:35.880
I, um, I'll get my breakfast on Tom this morning.

00:18:36.230 --> 00:18:40.250
Now, why would Wally want to carry messy old shoelaces like that in his pocket?

00:18:40.960 --> 00:18:42.080
Well, those are not shoelaces.

00:18:43.220 --> 00:18:44.840
I think they're very old worms.

00:18:46.900 --> 00:18:47.340
Worms?

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:49.680
Hey, what's this?

00:18:50.980 --> 00:18:52.580
Well, I found that in another pocket.

00:18:53.080 --> 00:18:54.840
Looks like some sort of a fang.

00:18:55.140 --> 00:18:55.960
Ah, it's a horse tooth.

00:18:58.010 --> 00:18:59.970
Now, why would anyone want a horse tooth?

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:03.460
Oh, I don't know. I just know I wouldn't trust a kid who didn't want one.

00:19:07.540 --> 00:19:10.490
Ward, look. Here's a notice from the boys' school.

00:19:10.750 --> 00:19:14.810
Grant Avenue School Band Concert, 7 p.m. Thursday night.

00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:16.200
Hey, that's tonight.

00:19:17.150 --> 00:19:22.530
Yes. I wonder why the beaver didn't show it to us. He's so proud of being in the band.

00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:26.210
Oh, he probably just stuck it in his pocket along with the horse tooth and forgot about it.

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.980
That's funny. Here it is, the beaver's big night.

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:31.420
and he doesn't even mention it.

00:19:31.520 --> 00:19:32.520
Ain't that just like it, kid?

00:19:33.840 --> 00:19:34.400
Goodbye, dear.

00:19:35.180 --> 00:19:36.020
Have a nice breakfast.

00:19:43.110 --> 00:19:45.390
Hey, Beebe, will you tell me something?

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:47.120
What are you doing?

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:50.360
Putting on my blue suit.

00:19:51.320 --> 00:19:53.620
What for? You can't go to the band concert.

00:19:53.820 --> 00:19:54.540
You're not in it.

00:19:57.960 --> 00:19:59.500
Mom and Dad think I'm in it.

00:20:00.250 --> 00:20:04.570
So you're just going to put on your blue suit and walk out of here with Mom and Dad with a clarinet under your arm?

00:20:06.810 --> 00:20:08.130
What else can I do?

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.100
Anyway, something good might happen on the way.

00:20:12.720 --> 00:20:13.520
Like what?

00:20:15.780 --> 00:20:17.160
Like I might get sick in the car.

00:20:17.940 --> 00:20:20.440
Beaver, a guy just can't get sick when he wants to.

00:20:22.750 --> 00:20:23.570
I guess not.

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:26.530
Can you think of anything, Wally?

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:29.370
Well.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:33.160
You couldn't play the clarinet if you wrecked your fingers.

00:20:34.300 --> 00:20:35.580
How could I wreck my fingers?

00:20:36.990 --> 00:20:38.910
Well, you could slam them in the door or something.

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:45.230
Wally, help me tie my tie.

00:20:47.090 --> 00:20:52.370
How come you're not getting dressed? Aren't you going?

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:56.060
No, I don't want to be there and watch what happens when they find out what you did.

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:58.420
Should have told them a long time ago.

00:20:59.260 --> 00:20:59.960
But that was so...

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:01.780
for getting on the baseball team.

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:05.300
I wanted him to have something to be proud of me for.

00:21:07.760 --> 00:21:09.080
Yeah, well...

00:21:10.800 --> 00:21:11.760
Wally, where you going?

00:21:12.650 --> 00:21:14.860
Uh, well, I gotta get some stuff downstairs.

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:21.790
Darling, what are you so nervous about?

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:23.250
You aren't gonna play tonight.

00:21:23.630 --> 00:21:26.050
Well, I certainly am. I'm gonna be right in there

00:21:26.050 --> 00:21:27.890
straining for every note with him.

00:21:34.350 --> 00:21:35.070
Where's the beaver?

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:36.830
He's not dressed.

00:21:37.440 --> 00:21:39.700
Well, he'll be right down in a couple minutes.

00:21:41.900 --> 00:21:43.260
I think there's something you ought to know.

00:21:44.340 --> 00:21:44.680
What?

00:21:45.700 --> 00:21:55.960
Well, uh, well, the beaver's not in the band.

00:21:56.120 --> 00:21:59.980
You mean he's not?

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:02.320
We're going to hear him play tonight.

00:22:03.290 --> 00:22:05.910
Well, Mr. Willop flunked him out of the band three weeks ago.

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.880
Oh, he's been taking his clarinet to school every morning.

00:22:11.680 --> 00:22:14.600
I know. He's been hoping something good would happen before he found out.

00:22:17.290 --> 00:22:20.210
Why would he deliberately try to deceive us like this?

00:22:22.510 --> 00:22:25.980
Well, you got such a kick out on me making the baseball team and everything.

00:22:26.280 --> 00:22:28.080
He wanted you to like him for something, too.

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:49.350
Well, Beaver, Beaver, something's come up and your mother and I are not going to be able to go to the concert.

00:22:53.640 --> 00:22:55.020
Awfully sorry to disappoint you.

00:22:56.180 --> 00:22:57.760
You mean I'm gonna have to go by myself?

00:23:00.410 --> 00:23:03.730
Well, I don't think a fellow your age should go out alone at night.

00:23:05.470 --> 00:23:09.670
Yes, Beaver, why don't you go upstairs and take your suit off and...

00:23:10.590 --> 00:23:12.630
Oh, maybe we can all go some other time.

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:16.500
Yeah, maybe we can go some other time.

00:23:22.420 --> 00:23:24.300
I don't think Mr. Will would really miss me.

00:23:24.790 --> 00:23:25.430
Hey, wait up, Bea.

00:23:25.950 --> 00:23:26.650
Sure, love.

00:23:37.800 --> 00:23:43.580
Can you imagine what he's gone through for the last few weeks just so you'd still be proud of him?

00:23:44.210 --> 00:23:48.010
Yep. You know something, June?

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:53.300
We'd be a lot better parents if we didn't wait till they did something good to let them know we're proud of them.

00:23:54.840 --> 00:23:59.370
Every once in a while, just for no reason at all, we ought to tell them we love them.

00:24:08.670 --> 00:24:10.970
I just wanted to remind you boys to brush your teeth

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:12.090
before you go to bed tonight.

00:24:12.380 --> 00:24:14.400
Your mother will be up in a few minutes, say good night to you.

00:24:14.530 --> 00:24:15.750
OK, Dad. Good night.

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:19.750
Good night, Wally. Good night, Bede.

00:24:21.250 --> 00:24:21.570
Dad?

00:24:23.060 --> 00:24:23.700
What is it, Bede?

00:24:31.240 --> 00:24:31.640
Thanks.

00:24:32.820 --> 00:24:34.100
Well, thanks for what, Beaver?

00:24:35.550 --> 00:24:37.890
Well, pretending you still think I was in the band.

00:24:39.360 --> 00:24:39.460
Oh.

00:24:41.130 --> 00:24:42.630
Well, don't you worry about it, Beaver.

00:24:43.790 --> 00:24:44.990
You'll make it next year.

00:25:01.310 --> 00:25:01.330
You

00:25:30.000 --> 00:25:37.340
Thank you.
